      "title": "The Sieve of Eratosthenes",
      "pages": [4, 5],
      "text": "The document describes the sieve of Eratosthenes as an old method for finding all primes up to a limit. It explains the process of circling numbers and crossing out multiples, highlighting that primes are the numbers that remain circled. It also discusses the efficiency of the sieve and its limitations regarding memory for very large ranges."
    },
    {
      "title": "Counting Primes",
      "pages": [5, 6],
      "text": "The document introduces the function pi(x), which counts the number of primes up to x. It provides a table of pi(x) values for powers of ten and compares them to the estimate x / ln(x), illustrating the thinning of primes as numbers increase. It also mentions Carl Friedrich Gauss's observation of this pattern."
    },
